> [!WARNING]
> **This chart is deprecated** and will be removed in the future. Please use the [official Passbolt chart](https://github.com/passbolt/charts-passbolt) instead:
>
> - it deploys a current Passbolt version, whereas this chart is bound to Passbolt 3.4.0 by the unmaintained [cnieg/passbolt](https://github.com/cnieg/helm-charts/tree/master/charts/passbolt) chart
> - it supports multiple Passbolt replicas with Redis, and MariaDB or PostgreSQL as database (bundled or external), so this chart doesn't add anything on top
>
> See [Migrating to the official Passbolt chart](#migrating-to-the-official-passbolt-chart).

## TL;DR;

```console
helm repo add christianhuth https://charts.christianhuth.de
helm repo update
helm install passbolt-ha christianhuth/passbolt-ha
```

## Introduction

This chart bootstraps [Passbolt](https://github.com/passbolt), [MYSQL by Bitnami](https://github.com/bitnami/charts/tree/master/bitnami/mysql) and [ProxySQL](https://github.com/flachesis/proxysql/tree/master/charts/proxysql) using the [Helm](https://helm.sh) package manager.

## Prerequisites

- Kubernetes 1.19+

## Installing the Chart

To install the chart with the release name `passbolt-ha`:

```console
helm repo add christianhuth https://charts.christianhuth.de
helm repo update
helm install passbolt-ha christianhuth/passbolt-ha
```

These commands deploy Skooner on the Kubernetes cluster in the default configuration. The [Values](#values) section lists the values that can be configured during installation.

> **Tip**: List all releases using `helm list`

## Uninstalling the Chart

To uninstall the `passbolt-ha` deployment:

```console
helm uninstall passbolt-ha
```

The command removes all the Kubernetes components associated with the chart and deletes the release.

{{ template "chart.valuesSection" . }}

Specify each parameter using the `--set key=value[,key=value]` argument to `helm install`.

Alternatively, a YAML file that specifies the values for the parameters can be provided while installing the chart. For example,

```console
helm install passbolt-ha -f values.yaml christianhuth/passbolt-ha
```

### Setting up the database connection

By default the passwords used to install MySQL are very weak. You should therefore create a Secret like the following:

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: passbolt-mysql
stringData:
  mysql-password: 36X79oYHQBD5C8aGT1Gqzm2jLK4BWPC0
  mysql-replication-password: UDWAZ2MHISu75YW34S08OB1POTvKoE69
  mysql-root-password: 24D1FOHKvDLwt5MA087QUVO36IjY9rRY
  username: passbolt
type: Opaque
```

Then update your values like this:

```yaml
mysql:
  auth:
    existingSecret: passbolt-mysql
proxysql:
  proxysql:
    mysql:
      users:
        - username: root
          password: $NEW_PASSWORD
        - username: passbolt
          password: $NEW_PASSWORD
```

## Migrating to the official Passbolt chart

The official chart is available at:

```console
helm repo add passbolt https://download.passbolt.com/charts/passbolt
helm repo update
```

To take your data along:

1. Dump the database:

   ```console
   kubectl exec passbolt-mysql-primary-0 -- sh -c 'mysqldump -u"$MYSQL_USER" -p"$(cat "$MYSQL_PASSWORD_FILE")" --no-tablespaces "$MYSQL_DATABASE"' > passbolt.sql
   ```

2. Copy the GPG server keys from the Passbolt volume and store them in a Secret for the official chart (keys `serverkey.asc` and `serverkey_private.asc`, used with `gpgExistingSecret`):

   ```console
   POD=$(kubectl get pods -l app.kubernetes.io/name=passbolt -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
   kubectl cp $POD:/etc/passbolt/gpg/serverkey.asc serverkey.asc
   kubectl cp $POD:/etc/passbolt/gpg/serverkey_private.asc serverkey_private.asc
   kubectl create secret generic passbolt-gpg --from-file=serverkey.asc --from-file=serverkey_private.asc
   ```

3. Install the official chart with `gpgExistingSecret: passbolt-gpg` and restore the dump into its database (or into your own database, e.g. a MariaDB instance managed by the [mariadb-operator](https://github.com/mariadb-operator/mariadb-operator)). A MySQL dump can be restored into MariaDB without changes.

4. Passbolt migrates the database from 3.4.0 to the current version on startup. Read the [Passbolt upgrade notes](https://www.passbolt.com/docs/hosting/update/) before, as this skips several major versions.

## Upgrading the Chart

### To 5.0.0

This major updates the MySQL subchart to its newest major, 14.0.0: [Here](https://github.com/bitnami/charts/tree/main/bitnami/mysql#to-1400) you can find more information about the changes introduced in that version.

### 3.0.0

This major updates the MySQL subchart to its newest major, 13.0.0. [Here](https://github.com/bitnami/charts/tree/main/bitnami/mysql#to-1300) you can find more information about the changes introduced in that version.

### 2.0.0

This major updates the MySQL subchart to its newest major, 12.2.0. [Here](https://github.com/bitnami/charts/tree/main/bitnami/mysql#to-1220) you can find more information about the changes introduced in that version.
